% Test: top-level type parameter in a procedure declaration
% EXPECT: well-typed (positive)
%
% Supersedes negative/type_def/merge_undefined_type.glp.
%
% Under the top-level-parameter rule (typed-program.md, clause-template
% paragraph: "an argument position whose type is a parameter"), a bare name at
% a top-level argument position that is not a defined type is a type parameter.
% A carried value with no constructor wrapper and no common supertype — e.g. a
% lib/ router's message argument — can only appear this way.  So a declaration
% like keep(M?, M) is a valid parameterised procedure, NOT an undefined-type
% error (the behaviour merge_undefined_type.glp used to assert).
%
% This pins two things:
%   1. the top-level parameter M is accepted — the declaration loads;
%   2. a caller instantiating M at a concrete type type-checks — call-site
%      instantiation binds M = Stream(Integer).
% A known type at a top-level position stays concrete, so monomorphic
% declarations are unaffected.  Per-instantiation defining-clause checking
% (Phase 2, project mode) is pinned by the negative routing fixture.

procedure keep(M?, M).
keep(X, X?).

procedure use_keep(Stream(Integer)?, Stream(Integer)).
use_keep(A, B?) :- keep(A?, B).
